WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.062 --> 00:00:04.969
That night Holmes and I set out on our adventure at precisely nine-thirty.

2
00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.144
Sitting next to him in a cab was just like old times.

3
00:00:08.145 --> 00:00:11.379
"Did you remember to bring your revolver?" Holmes asked.

4
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.518
"Of course." I patted my pocket to reassure myself that it was there.

5
00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:16.469
"Good."

6
00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:18.323
We rode along in silence.

7
00:00:18.324 --> 00:00:23.398
I could see that Holmes' brow was furrowed, but I knew better than to ask what he was thinking.

8
00:00:23.399 --> 00:00:25.385
All would be revealed eventually.

9
00:00:25.386 --> 00:00:31.311
We seemed to be headed toward our old rooms on Baker Street, so I was surprised when Holmes suddenly stopped the cab.

10
00:00:31.312 --> 00:00:35.154
We got out, and he looked around to be sure we weren't being followed.

11
00:00:35.155 --> 00:00:39.839
Then he led me through back alleys and stable yards that I'd never seen before.

12
00:00:40.517 --> 00:00:45.473
At last we stopped at the back door of a house, where Holmes let us in with a key.

13
00:00:45.474 --> 00:00:49.227
The place was pitch-dark, but I could tell that it was completely empty.

14
00:00:49.228 --> 00:00:56.011
The floorboards creaked under our feet, and my outstretched hand touched strips of wallpaper peeling from a wall.

15
00:00:56.012 --> 00:01:03.031
Holmes' cold, thin fingers encircled my wrist as he pulled me upstairs and into a large room at the front of the house.

16
00:01:03.032 --> 00:01:06.643
The light from a streetlamp shone dimly through a dirty window.

17
00:01:06.886 --> 00:01:10.289
"Do you know where we are?" Holmes said, letting go of my wrist.

18
00:01:10.455 --> 00:01:12.983
I peered through the window to the street below.

19
00:01:12.984 --> 00:01:16.101
"Surely this is Baker Street, but why are we here?"

20
00:01:16.102 --> 00:01:19.725
"Because this house commands a perfect view of our old rooms.

21
00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:25.763
Look across the street. Do you recognize that fellow sitting beside the window?"

22
00:01:25.764 --> 00:01:30.478
I gave a cry of amazement as I caught sight of a man silhouetted against the drawn blind. There

23
00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:33.369
was no mistaking that profile. "It's

24
00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:36.558
you! But how can that be?" Holmes

25
00:01:36.559 --> 00:01:38.779
was shaking with laughter. "Evidently,

26
00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:44.325
over the past three years, I have not lost my ability to surprise you, my dear Watson. It

27
00:01:44.326 --> 00:01:47.240
really is a remarkable likeness, isn't it? While

28
00:01:47.241 --> 00:01:54.087
in France I had a wax bust made of myself, and with Mrs. Hudson's help, I put it in place this afternoon." "Why

29
00:01:54.088 --> 00:01:56.237
go to all that trouble?" "Because

30
00:01:56.238 --> 00:02:00.910
I wished someone to think that I was there when I was really elsewhere." "And

31
00:02:00.911 --> 00:02:03.738
you thought your rooms were being watched?" "I

32
00:02:04.249 --> 00:02:06.347
knew they were being watched." Holmes

33
00:02:06.348 --> 00:02:12.569
said that, with the exception of one man, all of Professor Moriarty's men were either dead or in jail. "Knowing

34
00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:19.972
that I was still alive," Holmes said, "this particular man believed that sooner or later I would return to my rooms. And

35
00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:22.561
today his lookout saw me arrive." "How

36
00:02:22.562 --> 00:02:24.057
do you know that?" "Because

37
00:02:24.058 --> 00:02:29.847
when I glanced out my window, I recognized the lookout—a minor crook," Holmes said. "But

38
00:02:29.848 --> 00:02:31.865
I'm not worried about him. My

39
00:02:31.866 --> 00:02:40.109
main concern is the man who hired him, a very dangerous and cunning criminal, and also Moriarty's best friend and chief of staff. He's

40
00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:46.331
the one who witnessed my escape from the Reichenbach Falls and tried to kill me by dropping rocks over the cliff." I

41
00:02:46.885 --> 00:02:50.630
gasped. "And he's right here in London?" Holmes

42
00:02:50.631 --> 00:02:58.357
nodded. "That's the man who is after me tonight, Watson, and that's the man who is quite unaware that we are after him ."

43
00:02:58.358 --> 00:03:01.778
We stood together in the darkness, watching the people below us.

44
00:03:01.779 --> 00:03:08.557
I sensed that Holmes was keenly alert and noticed that his eyes were fixed intently on the stream of passersby.

45
00:03:08.558 --> 00:03:12.130
It was a bleak night with the wind whistling down the street.

46
00:03:12.131 --> 00:03:17.419
At one point I observed two men sheltering from the wind in the doorway of a nearby house.

47
00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:24.018
I tried to draw Holmes' attention to them, but he let out an exasperated sigh and continued staring out the window.

48
00:03:24.019 --> 00:03:27.700
More than once his fingers drummed against the wall.

49
00:03:28.137 --> 00:03:34.052
It was evident that the great detective was growing uneasy and that his plans were not working out as he had hoped.

50
00:03:34.053 --> 00:03:39.242
As midnight approached and the street became deserted, Holmes paced the room in agitation.

51
00:03:39.243 --> 00:03:43.536
I was about to make a remark when I noticed something in the lighted window across the street.

52
00:03:43.537 --> 00:03:48.634
"The silhouette on the blind has moved!" I clutched Holmes' arm and pointed.

53
00:03:48.635 --> 00:03:51.294
"Look! The back is facing us now!"

54
00:03:51.295 --> 00:03:53.880
"Of course it has moved," Holmes snapped.

55
00:03:53.881 --> 00:03:59.666
"Do you honestly think I'd expect one of the sharpest minds in England to be fooled by an immobile wax dummy?"

56
00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:06.942
Holmes' time away had obviously not improved his temper or reduced his impatience with people less intelligent than himself.

57
00:04:07.537 --> 00:04:10.705
"We've been in this room for two hours," Holmes went on.

58
00:04:10.706 --> 00:04:16.087
"Mrs. Hudson has made some small change to that figure eight times, or once every fifteen minutes.

59
00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:20.215
She's careful to make sure that her shadow never appears on the blind."

60
00:04:20.216 --> 00:04:25.179
Suddenly he pulled me into the blackest corner of the room and whispered in my ear to be quiet.

61
00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:29.216
The fingers that clutched my arm were quivering with suppressed excitement.

62
00:04:29.217 --> 00:04:34.039
In the next instant, I was aware of what his keener senses had already distinguished.

63
00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:39.771
A small noise reached my ears, not from the direction of Baker Street, but from the back of the house. I

64
00:04:39.772 --> 00:04:41.962
heard a door open and shut. Then

65
00:04:41.963 --> 00:04:46.641
I heard footsteps creeping up the stairs and down the hall toward us. The

66
00:04:47.217 --> 00:04:50.962
footsteps reverberated harshly throughout the empty house. Holmes

67
00:04:50.963 --> 00:04:56.351
crouched against the wall, and I did the same, my hand closing on my revolver. Peering

68
00:04:56.352 --> 00:05:00.977
through the gloom, I saw the vague outline of a man entering the room. The

69
00:05:00.978 --> 00:05:06.719
sinister figure was within ten feet of us before I realized that he had no idea of our presence. Stealing

70
00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.627
over to the window, he quietly raised it about half a foot. As

71
00:05:10.628 --> 00:05:14.632
he knelt on the floor, the beam from the streetlamp lit his face. He

72
00:05:14.633 --> 00:05:19.974
was an elderly man, wearing formal evening clothes, an overcoat, and a top hat. Instead

73
00:05:19.975 --> 00:05:25.309
of resembling a killer, he looked like a gentleman who’d spent the evening at the opera. I

74
00:05:25.995 --> 00:05:30.683
thought he was carrying a stick until he laid it on the floor with a metallic clang. When

75
00:05:30.684 --> 00:05:37.062
he pulled something from the pocket of his coat, I realized with horror he was assembling some kind of gun. For

76
00:05:37.063 --> 00:05:39.459
an instant the man was motionless. Then

77
00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:43.633
he cuddled the gun into his shoulder, and his finger tightened on the trigger. There

78
00:05:43.634 --> 00:05:47.971
was a strange whiz! , which didn't sound like any gunshot I'd ever heard.

79
00:05:47.972 --> 00:05:54.165
But the tinkle of broken glass confirmed that he'd hit his target—the wax dummy across the street!

80
00:05:54.166 --> 00:05:58.395
Holmes sprang like a tiger onto the man's back and wrestled him to the floor.

81
00:05:58.396 --> 00:06:01.752
The man jumped up and seized Holmes by the throat.

82
00:06:01.753 --> 00:06:06.441
"Holmes!" I cried as I rushed to his aid.

